# Hard Half — CONTEXT

## Working idea

A practice layer over an existing reference layer. The reference layer is the LLM coding
benchmark's published manuals (anthonykot.github.io/lc-benchmark-manuals): 37 recent LeetCode
weekly-contest problems, Q3 and Q4 only, each with a manual generated from every accepted
model solution. The practice layer is one page: a ladder of ten of those problems, ordered by
model acceptance rate, with a stopwatch, a self-recorded verdict, and, only after the attempt,
the models' mistakes and the manual.

The opponent is the published record of which models got Accepted. That is the stake the three
earlier AlgoPath attempts lacked (user diagnosis, 2026-09-08: "what was missing is either more
interesting/complex tasks or some gamification"). Here the hard content and the game are the
same feature.

## Evidence

- **User-reported (2026-09-08):** three published AlgoPath versions (v1 static handbook, v2
  Vite study app, v3 curriculum DAG) were not good enough to share; the missing thing was
  harder problems or gamification.
- **Observed:** the benchmark holds LeetCode ground truth for model submissions
  (`submissions/<model>/<problem>.json`, fields `accepted`, `status_msg`, `lang`) and manuals with
  a "Wrong But Tempting" section per problem.
- **Hypothesis:** a reader who sees "12 of 29 model submissions accepted" attempts the problem and
  returns for the next rung. Opening the page is not success; a second recorded attempt is.

## What this is not

- Not a curriculum, not spaced repetition, not an account system. Progress is browser-local.
- Not a new manual generator. Manuals are linked, not rewritten; the only lifted text is each
  manual's "Wrong But Tempting" section, shown after the attempt.
- Not a claim about model rankings. Counts are submissions (model × language), shown as such.

## Pipeline

    scripts/build_data.py   # reads the benchmark checkout (BENCH env var) → data/problems.json
    scripts/build.py        # data/problems.json + scripts/template.html → docs/index.html
    ./verify.sh             # data integrity, links, page sanity

The ladder is the LADDER list in `build_data.py`, then sorted by acceptance rate. Set 1 (WC482–489)
only, because set 2 has at most nine submissions per problem and the scoreboard would look thin.

## Reader test (the gate)

Put the page in front of one person preparing for interviews. Success = a second attempt recorded
on a later day. Record what they said about the challenge line, the reveal, and the manual link.
Do not add features before that result exists.
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Hard Half — beat the model on the hard half of LeetCode contests</title>
<meta name="description" content="Ten recent LeetCode weekly-contest problems, Q3 and Q4 only, with a stopwatch and the record of which AI models actually solved each one.">
<style>
:root{--ink:#1b1b1f;--muted:#5f6470;--bg:#f7f6f2;--card:#ffffff;--line:#e3e1da;--accent:#0b6e4f;--warn:#b2452b;--soft:#eef3f0;--mono:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace}
@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){:root{--ink:#e8e6e1;--muted:#a3a7b0;--bg:#15161a;--card:#1e2026;--line:#2e313a;--accent:#5ccf9f;--warn:#f08c6e;--soft:#23292a}}
*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink);font:16px/1.55 Georgia,"Times New Roman",serif}
.wrap{max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;padding:2rem 1.2rem 5rem}
h1{font-size:2.1rem;line-height:1.15;margin:.2rem 0 .6rem}h2{font-size:1.35rem;margin:0 0 .3rem}h3{font-size:1rem;margin:1.2rem 0 .4rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--muted)}
.lede{font-size:1.1rem;color:var(--muted);max-width:640px}
.rules{background:var(--soft);border:1px solid var(--line);padding:1rem 1.2rem;margin:1.4rem 0 2rem;border-radius:6px}
.rules ol{margin:.4rem 0 0 1.2rem;padding:0}
.ladder{list-style:none;padding:0;margin:0}
.card{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;padding:1.1rem 1.3rem;margin:0 0 1rem}
.card header{display:flex;gap:.8rem;align-items:baseline;flex-wrap:wrap}
.rank{font:700 .8rem var(--mono);color:var(--muted)}.diff{font:600 .72rem var(--mono);padding:.1rem .45rem;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:4px;color:var(--muted)}
.challenge{margin:.5rem 0 .2rem;font-weight:600}
.bar{height:8px;background:var(--line);border-radius:4px;overflow:hidden;margin:.3rem 0 .8rem}.bar i{display:block;height:100%;background:var(--accent)}
.chips{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.35rem;margin:.2rem 0 .6rem}
.chip{font:.74rem var(--mono);padding:.15rem .5rem;border-radius:999px;border:1px solid var(--line);color:var(--muted);cursor:default}
.chip.all{border-color:var(--accent);color:var(--accent)}.chip.none{border-color:var(--warn);color:var(--warn)}
details summary{cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent);font-weight:600;margin:.4rem 0}
.statement{font-size:.95rem}.statement pre{background:var(--soft);padding:.6rem .8rem;overflow:auto;border-radius:4px;font-size:.85rem}.statement code{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.9em}
.controls{display:flex;gap:.5rem;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:.8rem 0 .4rem}
button{font:600 .85rem system-ui,sans-serif;padding:.45rem .8rem;border-radius:6px;border:1px solid var(--line);background:var(--card);color:var(--ink);cursor:pointer}
button.primary{background:var(--accent);border-color:var(--accent);color:#fff}button:disabled{opacity:.45;cursor:default}
.clock{font:700 1.1rem var(--mono);min-width:5.5ch}
.verdict{font-size:.9rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.2rem 0}
.reveal{display:none;border-top:1px dashed var(--line);margin-top:.9rem;padding-top:.6rem}.card.done .reveal{display:block}
.reveal .tempting{font-size:.93rem}.reveal a{color:var(--accent)}
.card.done .attempt{display:none}
.summary{border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin-top:2rem;padding-top:1rem;color:var(--muted);font-size:.95rem}
.note{font-size:.85rem;color:var(--muted)}
a{color:var(--accent)}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main class="wrap">
<p class="note">Hard Half · pilot · 10 problems</p>
<h1>The models have already tried these. Your turn.</h1>
<p class="lede">Ten problems from the hard half of recent LeetCode weekly contests, questions 3 and 4 only. Every one was submitted to LeetCode by a dozen AI models in up to seven languages, and the record of who got <em>Accepted</em> is below each problem. Start the clock, solve it however you solve things, then record what happened. Only then do you get the models' mistakes and the full manual.</p>
<div class="rules">
<strong>How this works.</strong>
<ol>
<li>Pick the lowest rung you have not cleared. The ladder is ordered by how many model attempts were accepted, so the first win comes early and the last rung is the one most models failed.</li>
<li>Press <b>Start</b>, read, think, code wherever you like. Submit on LeetCode if you want a real verdict.</li>
<li>Press <b>Passed</b>, <b>Failed</b> or <b>Gave up</b>. Your time and verdict stay in this browser only.</li>
<li>The card then opens: what the models got wrong on this problem, and the link to the manual built from their accepted solutions.</li>
</ol>
</div>
<ol class="ladder" id="ladder"></ol>
<div class="summary" id="summary"></div>
<p class="note">Source of the model record: an independent benchmark that submitted model-written solutions to LeetCode and kept the verdicts. Counts are <em>submissions</em>, one per model and language, not distinct models. Manuals are generated from accepted solutions and are the reference, not the answer key. Problem statements are LeetCode's; links go to the original problems.</p>
</main>
<script>
const DATA = [{"rank": 1, "id": "wc0482-Q3-smallest-all-ones-multiple", "title": "Smallest All-Ones Multiple", "difficulty": "Medium", "contest": 482, "position": "Q3", "leetcode_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/smallest-all-ones-multiple/", "function": "minAllOneMultiple", "tags": ["Hash Table", "Math"], "statement_html": "<p>You are given a positive integer <code>k<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>Find the <strong>smallest<\/strong> integer <code>n<\/code> divisible by <code>k<\/code> that consists of <strong>only the digit 1<\/strong> in its decimal representation (e.g., 1, 11, 111, ...).<\/p>\n\n<p>Return an integer denoting the <strong>number of digits<\/strong> in the decimal representation of <code>n<\/code>. If no such <code>n<\/code> exists, return <code>-1<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 1:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">k = 3<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">3<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p><code>n = 111<\/code> because 111 is divisible by 3, but 1 and 11 are not. The length of <code>n = 111<\/code> is 3.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 2:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">k = 7<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">6<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p><code>n = 111111<\/code>. The length of <code>n = 111111<\/code> is 6.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 3:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">k = 2<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">-1<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>There does not exist a valid <code>n<\/code> that is a multiple of 2.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>2 &lt;= k &lt;= 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n", "essence_html": "<p>\n  A <em>repunit<\/em> is a number whose decimal representation consists entirely of 1s: 1, 11, 111, 1111, … .\n  Given a positive integer <code>k<\/code> (2 ≤ k ≤ 10<sup>5<\/sup>), find the smallest repunit\n  that is divisible by <code>k<\/code> and return its number of digits.\n<\/p>\n<p>\n  If <code>k<\/code> has 2 or 5 as a factor, no repunit can ever be divisible by it — repunits are always odd\n  and end in 1, so they are coprime to both 2 and 5 — and the answer is <code>-1<\/code>.\n  For all other <code>k<\/code>, a solution always exists and can be found by tracking remainders\n  modulo <code>k<\/code> as digits are appended, without ever building the large number itself.\n<\/p>\n\n<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n     4. WORKED EXAMPLE\n     ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->", "tempting_html": "<div class=\"wrong-card\">\n  <h4>① Build the repunit as a big integer directly<\/h4>\n  <p>\n    Generate <code>n = 1<\/code>, <code>n = 11<\/code>, <code>n = 111<\/code>, … and test <code>n % k == 0<\/code>\n    using Python's arbitrary-precision ints or Java's <code>BigInteger<\/code>.\n    Each iteration the number grows by one digit, so <code>n % k<\/code> on a <em>d<\/em>-digit number\n    costs O(<em>d<\/em>) big-integer work.  Over <em>k<\/em> steps: O(k) × O(k) = <strong>O(k²) = 10<sup>10<\/sup> ops<\/strong>\n    for k = 10<sup>5<\/sup>. TLE. The fix is to keep only the remainder — which costs O(1) per step.\n  <\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"wrong-card\">\n  <h4>② Iterate multiples of k, check if all digits are 1<\/h4>\n  <p>\n    For each multiple <code>m = k, 2k, 3k, …<\/code> convert to string and check <code>all(c == '1' for c in str(m))<\/code>.\n    The smallest repunit divisible by k has up to k digits, so it can be as large as R(k) = (10<sup>k<\/sup> − 1) / 9.\n    The number of multiples to check before reaching R(k) is R(k) / k ≈ 10<sup>k<\/sup> / (9k) — astronomically\n    large even for k = 10. <strong>Completely intractable<\/strong>.\n  <\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"wrong-card\">\n  <h4>③ Forget the 2/5 guard and rely only on the loop<\/h4>\n  <p>\n    For k = 2: the remainder sequence is 1, 1, 1, … (period 1, never 0).\n    A loop without a 2/5 check must run all k = 2 iterations and return -1 — <em>happens to work<\/em>\n    because the loop bound is k.  But for k = 10 the sequence is 1, 1, 1, … (period 1 again)\n    and the loop still terminates correctly after k steps.  However, omitting the guard loses\n    an O(1) early-exit that has no downside: the guard is always cheaper than even one loop\n    iteration. More importantly, skipping it is confusing to readers and causes a 6× runtime\n    penalty in practice (see <em>glm-5-cloud-t0.8-java<\/em> at 50 ms vs 8 ms median).\n  <\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n     9. ACCEPTED ALGORITHM FAMILIES\n     ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->", "manual_url": "manuals/wc0482-Q3-smallest-all-ones-multiple.html", "models": [{"family": "claude", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "codex", "attempts": 5, "accepted": 5, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 4, "attempts": 4}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "deepseek", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "javascript": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 3, "attempts": 3}}}, {"family": "glm", "attempts": 6, "accepted": 6, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "rust": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "kimi", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"cpp": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "rust": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "typescript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "minimax", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "qwen", "attempts": 8, "accepted": 8, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 3, "attempts": 3}, "python3": {"accepted": 4, "attempts": 4}}}], "accepted": 39, "attempts": 39, "rate": 1.0}, {"rank": 2, "id": "wc0484-Q3-count-caesar-cipher-pairs", "title": "Count Caesar Cipher Pairs", "difficulty": "Medium", "contest": 484, "position": "Q3", "leetcode_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-caesar-cipher-pairs/", "function": "countPairs", "tags": ["Array", "Hash Table", "Math", "String", "Counting"], "statement_html": "<p>You are given an array <code>words<\/code> of <code>n<\/code> strings. Each string has length <code>m<\/code> and contains only lowercase English letters.<\/p>\n\n<p>Two strings <code>s<\/code> and <code>t<\/code> are <strong>similar<\/strong> if we can apply the following operation any number of times (possibly zero times) so that <code>s<\/code> and <code>t<\/code> become <strong>equal<\/strong>.<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Choose either <code>s<\/code> or <code>t<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>Replace <strong>every<\/strong> letter in the chosen string with the next letter in the alphabet cyclically. The next letter after <code>&#39;z&#39;<\/code> is <code>&#39;a&#39;<\/code>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>Count the number of pairs of indices <code>(i, j)<\/code> such that:<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>i &lt; j<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>words[i]<\/code> and <code>words[j]<\/code> are <strong>similar<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>Return an integer denoting the number of such pairs.<\/p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 1:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">words = [&quot;fusion&quot;,&quot;layout&quot;]<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">1<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p><code>words[0] = &quot;fusion&quot;<\/code> and <code>words[1] = &quot;layout&quot;<\/code> are similar because we can apply the operation to <code>&quot;fusion&quot;<\/code> 6 times. The string <code>&quot;fusion&quot;<\/code> changes as follows.<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>&quot;fusion&quot;<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>&quot;gvtjpo&quot;<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>&quot;hwukqp&quot;<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>&quot;ixvlrq&quot;<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>&quot;jywmsr&quot;<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>&quot;kzxnts&quot;<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>&quot;layout&quot;<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 2:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">words = [&quot;ab&quot;,&quot;aa&quot;,&quot;za&quot;,&quot;aa&quot;]<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">2<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p><code>words[0] = &quot;ab&quot;<\/code> and <code>words[2] = &quot;za&quot;<\/code> are similar. <code>words[1] = &quot;aa&quot;<\/code> and <code>words[3] = &quot;aa&quot;<\/code> are similar.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= n == words.length &lt;= 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= m == words[i].length &lt;= 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= n * m &lt;= 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>words[i]<\/code> consists only of lowercase English letters.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n", "essence_html": "<div class=\"card\">\n      <p>You are given <code>n<\/code> strings, each of the same length <code>m<\/code>. Two strings are <strong>similar<\/strong> if one can be turned into the other by repeatedly shifting every character forward by one (cyclically, <code>'z'<\/code> wraps to <code>'a'<\/code>)&mdash;in other words, if they are Caesar-cipher rotations of each other.<\/p>\n      <p style=\"margin-top:10px\">Count the number of ordered pairs <code>(i, j)<\/code> with <code>i &lt; j<\/code> where <code>words[i]<\/code> and <code>words[j]<\/code> are similar.<\/p>\n      <p style=\"margin-top:10px\">The key insight is that two strings are similar if and only if they share the same <em>relative character structure<\/em>&mdash;the pattern of inter-character gaps mod 26 is shift-invariant, so it uniquely identifies a Caesar equivalence class.<\/p>\n      <p style=\"margin-top:10px\">The constraint <code>n&thinsp;&times;&thinsp;m &le; 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code> means the total character count is small regardless of the individual values of <code>n<\/code> and <code>m<\/code>, so an O(n&thinsp;&times;&thinsp;m) solution processes everything in one linear pass.<\/p>\n    <\/div>\n  <\/section>\n\n  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════\n       4. WORKED EXAMPLE\n       ═══════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <section class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"worked-example\">", "tempting_html": "<div class=\"card\">\n\n      <div class=\"tempt-card\">\n        <h3>1. O(n²) brute-force pair comparison<\/h3>\n        <p>For every pair <code>(i, j)<\/code>, try all 26 shifts of <code>words[i]<\/code> and check equality with <code>words[j]<\/code>. Complexity: <code>O(n² &times; 26 &times; m)<\/code>. With <code>n = 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code> (which is possible when <code>m = 1<\/code>), this gives <code>(10<sup>5<\/sup>)² &times; 26 = 2.6 &times; 10<sup>11<\/sup><\/code> operations — orders of magnitude too slow. The n×m constraint does not protect the n² factor.<\/p>\n      <\/div>\n\n      <div class=\"tempt-card\">\n        <h3>2. Grouping words by their first character<\/h3>\n        <p>Count pairs only among words starting with the same letter, reasoning that a Caesar shift preserves the first character. This is incorrect: applying a non-zero shift changes the first character. For example, <code>\"fusion\"<\/code> (starts with 'f') and <code>\"layout\"<\/code> (starts with 'l') are similar via shift 6, but are in different groups. This approach would give 0 pairs for the first sample instead of 1.<\/p>\n      <\/div>\n\n      <div class=\"tempt-card\">\n        <h3>3. Consecutive differences without mod 26<\/h3>\n        <p>Store signed differences <code>(word[i+1] &minus; word[i])<\/code> as the key. This fails on wrap-around pairs. For instance, <code>\"za\"<\/code> has signed difference <code>a &minus; z = &minus;25<\/code>, while <code>\"ab\"<\/code> has difference <code>b &minus; a = +1<\/code>. Without <code>mod 26<\/code>, <code>&minus;25 &ne; 1<\/code>, so the pair is missed — but <code>(&minus;25 + 26) mod 26 = 1<\/code> reveals they are in the same class. Sample 2 would return 1 instead of 2.<\/p>\n      <\/div>\n\n    <\/div>\n  <\/section>\n\n  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════\n       9. ACCEPTED ALGORITHM FAMILIES\n       ═══════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <section class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"families\">", "manual_url": "manuals/wc0484-Q3-count-caesar-cipher-pairs.html", "models": [{"family": "claude", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "codex", "attempts": 4, "accepted": 4, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 4, "attempts": 4}}}, {"family": "deepseek", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "javascript": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 3, "attempts": 3}}}, {"family": "glm", "attempts": 4, "accepted": 4, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "kimi", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"cpp": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "rust": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "typescript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "minimax", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "qwen", "attempts": 8, "accepted": 8, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 3, "attempts": 3}, "python3": {"accepted": 4, "attempts": 4}}}], "accepted": 36, "attempts": 36, "rate": 1.0}, {"rank": 3, "id": "wc0486-Q3-pythagorean-distance-nodes-in-a-tree", "title": "Pythagorean Distance Nodes in a Tree", "difficulty": "Medium", "contest": 486, "position": "Q3", "leetcode_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/pythagorean-distance-nodes-in-a-tree/", "function": "specialNodes", "tags": ["Tree", "Breadth-First Search"], "statement_html": "<p>You are given an integer <code>n<\/code> and an undirected tree with <code>n<\/code> nodes numbered from 0 to <code>n - 1<\/code>. The tree is represented by a 2D array <code>edges<\/code> of length <code>n - 1<\/code>, where <code>edges[i] = [u<sub>i<\/sub>, v<sub>i<\/sub>]<\/code> indicates an undirected edge between <code>u<sub>i<\/sub><\/code> and <code>v<sub>i<\/sub><\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>You are also given three <strong>distinct<\/strong> target nodes <code>x<\/code>, <code>y<\/code>, and <code>z<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>For any node <code>u<\/code> in the tree:<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Let <code>dx<\/code> be the distance from <code>u<\/code> to node <code>x<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li>Let <code>dy<\/code> be the distance from <code>u<\/code> to node <code>y<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li>Let <code>dz<\/code> be the distance from <code>u<\/code> to node <code>z<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>The node <code>u<\/code> is called <strong>special<\/strong> if the three distances form a <strong>Pythagorean Triplet<\/strong>.<\/p>\n\n<p>Return an integer denoting the number of special nodes in the tree.<\/p>\n\n<p>A <strong>Pythagorean triplet<\/strong> consists of three integers <code>a<\/code>, <code>b<\/code>, and <code>c<\/code> which, when sorted in <strong>ascending<\/strong> order, satisfy <code>a<sup>2<\/sup> + b<sup>2<\/sup> = c<sup>2<\/sup><\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>The <strong>distance<\/strong> between two nodes in a tree is the number of edges on the unique path between them.<\/p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 1:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">n = 4, edges = [[0,1],[0,2],[0,3]], x = 1, y = 2, z = 3<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">3<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>For each node, we compute its distances to nodes <code>x = 1<\/code>, <code>y = 2<\/code>, and <code>z = 3<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Node 0 has distances 1, 1, and 1. After sorting, the distances are 1, 1, and 1, which do not satisfy the Pythagorean condition.<\/li>\n\t<li>Node 1 has distances 0, 2, and 2. After sorting, the distances are 0, 2, and 2. Since <code>0<sup>2<\/sup> + 2<sup>2<\/sup> = 2<sup>2<\/sup><\/code>, node 1 is special.<\/li>\n\t<li>Node 2 has distances 2, 0, and 2. After sorting, the distances are 0, 2, and 2. Since <code>0<sup>2<\/sup> + 2<sup>2<\/sup> = 2<sup>2<\/sup><\/code>, node 2 is special.<\/li>\n\t<li>Node 3 has distances 2, 2, and 0. After sorting, the distances are 0, 2, and 2. This also satisfies the Pythagorean condition.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>Therefore, nodes 1, 2, and 3 are special, and the answer is 3.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 2:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">n = 4, edges = [[0,1],[1,2],[2,3]], x = 0, y = 3, z = 2<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">0<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>For each node, we compute its distances to nodes <code>x = 0<\/code>, <code>y = 3<\/code>, and <code>z = 2<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Node 0 has distances 0, 3, and 2. After sorting, the distances are 0, 2, and 3, which do not satisfy the Pythagorean condition.<\/li>\n\t<li>Node 1 has distances 1, 2, and 1. After sorting, the distances are 1, 1, and 2, which do not satisfy the Pythagorean condition.<\/li>\n\t<li>Node 2 has distances 2, 1, and 0. After sorting, the distances are 0, 1, and 2, which do not satisfy the Pythagorean condition.<\/li>\n\t<li>Node 3 has distances 3, 0, and 1. After sorting, the distances are 0, 1, and 3, which do not satisfy the Pythagorean condition.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>No node satisfies the Pythagorean condition. Therefore, the answer is 0.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 3:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">n = 4, edges = [[0,1],[1,2],[1,3]], x = 1, y = 3, z = 0<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">1<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>For each node, we compute its distances to nodes <code>x = 1<\/code>, <code>y = 3<\/code>, and <code>z = 0<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Node 0 has distances 1, 2, and 0. After sorting, the distances are 0, 1, and 2, which do not satisfy the Pythagorean condition.<\/li>\n\t<li>Node 1 has distances 0, 1, and 1. After sorting, the distances are 0, 1, and 1. Since <code>0<sup>2<\/sup> + 1<sup>2<\/sup> = 1<sup>2<\/sup><\/code>, node 1 is special.<\/li>\n\t<li>Node 2 has distances 1, 2, and 2. After sorting, the distances are 1, 2, and 2, which do not satisfy the Pythagorean condition.<\/li>\n\t<li>Node 3 has distances 1, 0, and 2. After sorting, the distances are 0, 1, and 2, which do not satisfy the Pythagorean condition.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>Therefore, the answer is 1.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>4 &lt;= n &lt;= 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>edges.length == n - 1<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>edges[i] = [u<sub>i<\/sub>, v<sub>i<\/sub>]<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>0 &lt;= u<sub>i<\/sub>, v<sub>i<\/sub>, x, y, z &lt;= n - 1<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>x<\/code>, <code>y<\/code>, and <code>z<\/code> are pairwise <strong>distinct<\/strong>.<\/li>\n\t<li>The input is generated such that <code>edges<\/code> represent a valid tree.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n", "essence_html": "<p>\n    You are given an undirected tree on <code>n<\/code> nodes (4 &lt;= n &lt;= 100000) and three distinct\n    target nodes <code>x<\/code>, <code>y<\/code>, <code>z<\/code>. For every node <code>u<\/code>, compute\n    the three hop-count distances <code>dx<\/code>, <code>dy<\/code>, <code>dz<\/code> to the targets. Node\n    <code>u<\/code> is \"special\" when those three distances, sorted ascending as <code>(a, b, c)<\/code>,\n    satisfy <code>a*a + b*b == c*c<\/code> (a Pythagorean triplet).\n  <\/p>\n  <p>\n    The degenerate case <code>(0, k, k)<\/code> is valid -- any target node itself may be special if the\n    other two targets are equidistant from it. Distances can reach <code>n-1 = 99999<\/code>, so\n    <code>a*a + b*b<\/code> can reach about 2e10, which requires 64-bit integers in compiled languages.\n  <\/p>\n<\/section>\n\n<!-- ===================== 4. WORKED EXAMPLE ===================== -->\n<section>", "tempting_html": "<div class=\"wrong-card\">\n    <strong>Approach 1: All-pairs BFS (O(n^2))<\/strong>\n    <p>\n      Run BFS from every node to find distances to x, y, z. Complexity:\n      n * O(n) = O(n^2). For n = 1e5: 1e5 * 1e5 = <strong>1e10 operations<\/strong>. Hard TLE.\n      The fix is obvious once you notice that the three target distances can be gathered with\n      exactly 3 BFS runs instead of n.\n    <\/p>\n  <\/div>\n\n  <div class=\"wrong-card\">\n    <strong>Approach 2: Recursive DFS for distances (stack overflow)<\/strong>\n    <p>\n      A recursive DFS to fill distances works on small inputs but fails on worst-case inputs.\n      A path graph with n = 1e5 creates a recursion depth of 1e5. Python's default recursion\n      limit is 1000; even with <code>sys.setrecursionlimit<\/code> the C stack overflows.\n      Use iterative BFS (deque) or an explicit stack-based DFS.\n    <\/p>\n  <\/div>\n\n  <div class=\"wrong-card\">\n    <strong>Approach 3: Integer overflow with int32 arithmetic<\/strong>\n    <p>\n      Using plain <code>int<\/code> in Java or <code>int<\/code> in C++ for the Pythagorean check.\n      Max distance = n-1 = 99999. Then a^2 + b^2 &lt;= 2 * 99999^2 = 19999600002,\n      which exceeds int32 max (2147483647 ~ 2.1e9) by roughly <strong>9x<\/strong>. The result\n      silently wraps or saturates, producing wrong answers only on large inputs where the test\n      infrastructure has long chains. The fix: cast to long/int64 before multiplying.\n    <\/p>\n  <\/div>\n<\/section>\n\n<!-- ===================== 9. ACCEPTED ALGORITHM FAMILIES ===================== -->\n<section>", "manual_url": "manuals/wc0486-Q3-pythagorean-distance-nodes-in-a-tree.html", "models": [{"family": "claude", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "codex", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 3, "attempts": 3}}}, {"family": "deepseek", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "javascript": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 3, "attempts": 3}}}, {"family": "glm", "attempts": 4, "accepted": 4, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "kimi", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"cpp": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "rust": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "typescript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "minimax", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "qwen", "attempts": 8, "accepted": 8, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 3, "attempts": 3}, "python3": {"accepted": 4, "attempts": 4}}}], "accepted": 35, "attempts": 35, "rate": 1.0}, {"rank": 4, "id": "wc0483-Q3-minimum-cost-to-make-two-binary-strings-equal", "title": "Minimum Cost to Make Two Binary Strings Equal", "difficulty": "Medium", "contest": 483, "position": "Q3", "leetcode_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-cost-to-make-two-binary-strings-equal/", "function": "minimumCost", "tags": ["String", "Greedy"], "statement_html": "<p>You are given two binary strings <code>s<\/code> and <code>t<\/code>, both of length <code>n<\/code>, and three <strong>positive<\/strong> integers <code>flipCost<\/code>, <code>swapCost<\/code>, and <code>crossCost<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>You are allowed to apply the following operations any number of times (in any order) to the strings <code>s<\/code> and <code>t<\/code>:<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Choose any index <code>i<\/code> and flip <code>s[i]<\/code> or <code>t[i]<\/code> (change <code>&#39;0&#39;<\/code> to <code>&#39;1&#39;<\/code> or <code>&#39;1&#39;<\/code> to <code>&#39;0&#39;<\/code>). The cost of this operation is <code>flipCost<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>Choose two <strong>distinct<\/strong> indices <code>i<\/code> and <code>j<\/code>, and swap either <code>s[i]<\/code> and <code>s[j]<\/code> or <code>t[i]<\/code> and <code>t[j]<\/code>. The cost of this operation is <code>swapCost<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>Choose an index <code>i<\/code> and swap <code>s[i]<\/code> with <code>t[i]<\/code>. The cost of this operation is <code>crossCost<\/code>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>Return an integer denoting the <strong>minimum<\/strong> total cost needed to make the strings <code>s<\/code> and <code>t<\/code> equal.<\/p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 1:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">s = &quot;01000&quot;, t = &quot;10111&quot;, flipCost = 10, swapCost = 2, crossCost = 2<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">16<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>We can perform the following operations:<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Swap <code>s[0]<\/code> and <code>s[1]<\/code> (<code>swapCost = 2<\/code>). After this operation, <code>s = &quot;10000&quot;<\/code> and <code>t = &quot;10111&quot;<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>Cross swap <code>s[2]<\/code> and <code>t[2]<\/code> (<code>crossCost = 2<\/code>). After this operation, <code>s = &quot;10100&quot;<\/code> and <code>t = &quot;10011&quot;<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>Swap <code>s[2]<\/code> and <code>s[3]<\/code> (<code>swapCost = 2<\/code>). After this operation, <code>s = &quot;10010&quot;<\/code> and <code>t = &quot;10011&quot;<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>Flip <code>s[4]<\/code> (<code>flipCost = 10<\/code>). After this operation, <code>s = t = &quot;10011&quot;<\/code>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>The total cost is <code>2 + 2 + 2 + 10 = 16<\/code>.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 2:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">s = &quot;001&quot;, t = &quot;110&quot;, flipCost = 2, swapCost = 100, crossCost = 100<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">6<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>Flipping all the bits of <code>s<\/code> makes the strings equal, and the total cost is <code>3 * flipCost = 3 * 2 = 6<\/code>.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 3:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">s = &quot;1010&quot;, t = &quot;1010&quot;, flipCost = 5, swapCost = 5, crossCost = 5<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">0<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>The strings are already equal, so no operations are required.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>n == s.length == t.length<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= n &lt;= 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code>​​​​​​​<\/li>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= flipCost, swapCost, crossCost &lt;= 10<sup>9<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>s<\/code> and <code>t<\/code> consist only of the characters <code>&#39;0&#39;<\/code> and <code>&#39;1&#39;<\/code>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n", "essence_html": "<p>Given two binary strings <code>s<\/code> and <code>t<\/code> of equal length, you must make them equal using three cost-bearing operations: flip any single bit in either string (cost <code>flipCost<\/code>), swap two positions <em>within<\/em> the same string (cost <code>swapCost<\/code>), or swap <code>s[i]<\/code> with <code>t[i]<\/code> at the same index across strings (cost <code>crossCost<\/code>).<\/p>\n  <p>Only positions where <code>s[i] ≠ t[i]<\/code> matter: positions where the strings already agree need no work. Each differing position is either a <strong>Type A<\/strong> mismatch (<code>s='0', t='1'<\/code>) or a <strong>Type B<\/strong> mismatch (<code>s='1', t='0'<\/code>); the key insight is that a within-string swap can simultaneously fix one Type A and one Type B position, while two same-type mismatches require an additional cross-swap to create an opposite pair first.<\/p>\n  <p>The problem reduces entirely to counting how many Type A and Type B mismatches exist, then greedily choosing the cheapest repair strategy for each category of pair, with no need to track which specific positions are involved.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<!-- ===================================================================\n     4. WORKED EXAMPLE\n     =================================================================== -->\n<div class=\"section\">", "tempting_html": "<div class=\"wrong-item\">\n    <div class=\"wrong-title\">1. Flip every mismatch individually<\/div>\n    <p>Cost = <code>(a + b) × flipCost<\/code>. This is an O(n) upper bound but ignores paired operations entirely. When <code>swapCost = 1<\/code> and <code>flipCost = 100<\/code> with <code>a = b = 50000<\/code>, the naive cost is 10⁷ while the correct answer is 50000 × 1 = 50000 — 200× too expensive. The formula happens to be correct only when <code>swapCost ≥ 2·flipCost<\/code> and <code>crossCost + swapCost ≥ 2·flipCost<\/code> simultaneously.<\/p>\n  <\/div>\n\n  <div class=\"wrong-item\">\n    <div class=\"wrong-title\">2. DP over positions tracking a running imbalance<\/div>\n    <p>A natural DP state might be <code>(position i, net imbalance a−b seen so far)<\/code>, trying to decide at each step which operation to apply. This creates O(n) positions × O(n) imbalance values = O(n²) = 10¹⁰ states for n = 10⁵ — far too slow. The key reduction is that the specific positions of each mismatch type are interchangeable (any Type A can pair with any Type B), so only the global counts <code>a<\/code> and <code>b<\/code> matter, collapsing the entire problem to four arithmetic operations on two integers.<\/p>\n  <\/div>\n\n  <div class=\"wrong-item\">\n    <div class=\"wrong-title\">3. Always use cross+swap to fix same-type pairs, never flip<\/div>\n    <p>A solver might see that a cross swap converts Type A → Type B, enabling an opposite-pair swap, and use this unconditionally for all same-type pairs. When <code>crossCost = 10⁹, swapCost = 10⁹, flipCost = 1<\/code>, this approach pays <code>2 × 10⁹<\/code> per pair of same-type mismatches, while the correct answer is <code>2·flipCost = 2<\/code> — off by a factor of 10⁹. The fix is the single <code>min(crossCost + swapCost, 2·flipCost)<\/code> comparison. Arithmetic: n = 10⁵ same-type pairs × 2×10⁹ each = 2×10¹⁴, vs correct 10⁵ × 2 = 2×10⁵.<\/p>\n  <\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<!-- ===================================================================\n     9. ACCEPTED ALGORITHM FAMILIES\n     =================================================================== -->\n<div class=\"section\">", "manual_url": "manuals/wc0483-Q3-minimum-cost-to-make-two-binary-strings-equal.html", "models": [{"family": "codex", "attempts": 2, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "deepseek", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "javascript": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 3, "attempts": 3}}}, {"family": "glm", "attempts": 4, "accepted": 4, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "kimi", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"cpp": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "rust": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "typescript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "minimax", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "qwen", "attempts": 4, "accepted": 4, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "claude", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}], "accepted": 29, "attempts": 30, "rate": 0.967}, {"rank": 5, "id": "wc0487-Q4-longest-alternating-subarray-after-removing-at-most-one-element", "title": "Longest Alternating Subarray After Removing At Most One Element", "difficulty": "Hard", "contest": 487, "position": "Q4", "leetcode_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-alternating-subarray-after-removing-at-most-one-element/", "function": "longestAlternating", "tags": ["Array", "Dynamic Programming", "Enumeration"], "statement_html": "<p>You are given an integer array <code>nums<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>A <span data-keyword=\"subarray\">subarray<\/span> <code>nums[l..r]<\/code> is <strong>alternating<\/strong> if one of the following holds:<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>nums[l] &lt; nums[l + 1] &gt; nums[l + 2] &lt; nums[l + 3] &gt; ...<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>nums[l] &gt; nums[l + 1] &lt; nums[l + 2] &gt; nums[l + 3] &lt; ...<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>In other words, if we compare adjacent elements in the subarray, then the comparisons alternate between <strong>strictly<\/strong> greater and <strong>strictly<\/strong> smaller.<\/p>\n\n<p>You can remove <strong>at most one<\/strong> element from <code>nums<\/code>. Then, you select an alternating subarray from <code>nums<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>Return an integer denoting the <strong>maximum<\/strong> <strong>length<\/strong> of the alternating subarray you can select.<\/p>\n\n<p>A subarray of length 1 is considered alternating.<\/p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 1:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">nums = [2,1,3,2]<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">4<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Choose not to remove elements.<\/li>\n\t<li>Select the entire array <code>[<u><strong>2, 1, 3, 2<\/strong><\/u>]<\/code>, which is alternating because <code>2 &gt; 1 &lt; 3 &gt; 2<\/code>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 2:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">nums = [3,2,1,2,3,2,1]<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">4<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Choose to remove <code>nums[3]<\/code> i.e., <code>[3, 2, 1, <u><strong>2<\/strong><\/u>, 3, 2, 1]<\/code>. The array becomes <code>[3, 2, 1, 3, 2, 1]<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>Select the subarray <code>[3, <strong><u>2, 1, 3, 2<\/u><\/strong>, 1]<\/code>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 3:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">nums = [100000,100000]<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">1<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Choose not to remove elements.<\/li>\n\t<li>Select the subarray <code>[100000, <u><strong>100000<\/strong><\/u>]<\/code>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>2 &lt;= nums.length &lt;= 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= nums[i] &lt;= 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n", "essence_html": "<p>\n  You are given an integer array <code>nums<\/code>. A subarray is <em>alternating<\/em>\n  if consecutive pairs strictly alternate between &lt; and &gt; -- equal adjacent elements\n  are forbidden. You may remove <strong>at most one<\/strong> element from the original array,\n  then pick any alternating contiguous subarray from the result. Return the maximum length\n  of such a subarray.\n<\/p>\n<p>\n  The key difficulty is that removing one element can bridge two previously incompatible\n  alternating segments if and only if the new adjacency (between the elements flanking the\n  removed one) continues the alternating pattern of both sides. A length-1 subarray is\n  always valid, so the answer is at least 1.\n<\/p>\n\n<!-- ============================================================\n     4. WORKED EXAMPLE + OTHER OFFICIAL EXAMPLES\n     ============================================================ -->", "tempting_html": "<h3>1. Brute-force: try every deletion then every subarray<\/h3>\n<p>\n  For each of the n deletion choices, scan all O(n^2) subarrays for alternation.\n  Total: n * n^2 = n^3. At n = 1e5: 1e5 * 1e10 = 1e15 ops. Unacceptably slow by\n  a factor of ~1e9.\n<\/p>\n\n<h3>2. Single sliding window without DP precomputation<\/h3>\n<p>\n  Attempt to slide a window tracking \"have I used my one skip?\" at each failure point.\n  This works for many simpler deletion problems but fails here because after skipping\n  an element, the two flanking elements must form a valid bridge with the existing\n  alternating prefix <em>and<\/em> suffix independently -- a single running state cannot\n  capture both sides simultaneously. Wrong answers appear on inputs like [3,2,1,2,3,2,1]\n  where the deletion is not at the first failure site.\n<\/p>\n\n<h3>3. O(n^2) -- for each right endpoint, binary-search or linear-scan for best left<\/h3>\n<p>\n  Try all pairs (l, r) with at most one deletion inside. For each right endpoint,\n  scanning left is O(n); total O(n^2). At n = 1e5: 1e10 ops, still ~1e4x too slow\n  for a 2-second time limit. The required approach is O(n) via the DP precomputation.\n<\/p>\n\n<!-- ============================================================\n     9. ACCEPTED ALGORITHM FAMILIES\n     ============================================================ -->", "manual_url": "manuals/wc0487-Q4-longest-alternating-subarray-after-removing-at-most-one-element.html", "models": [{"family": "codex", "attempts": 1, "accepted": 1, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "kimi", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"cpp": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "rust": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "typescript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "minimax", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "qwen", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "deepseek", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 5, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 2}, "javascript": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 3}}}, {"family": "glm", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}], "accepted": 21, "attempts": 24, "rate": 0.875}, {"rank": 6, "id": "wc0482-Q4-number-of-balanced-integers-in-a-range", "title": "Number of Balanced Integers in a Range", "difficulty": "Hard", "contest": 482, "position": "Q4", "leetcode_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-balanced-integers-in-a-range/", "function": "countBalanced", "tags": ["Dynamic Programming"], "statement_html": "<p>You are given two integers <code>low<\/code> and <code>high<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>An integer is called <strong>balanced<\/strong> if it satisfies <strong>both<\/strong> of the following conditions:<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>It contains <strong>at least<\/strong> two digits.<\/li>\n\t<li>The <strong>sum of digits at even positions<\/strong> is equal to the <strong>sum of digits at odd positions<\/strong> (the leftmost digit has position 1).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>Return an integer representing the number of balanced integers in the range <code>[low, high]<\/code> (both inclusive).<\/p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 1:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">low = 1, high = 100<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">9<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>The 9 balanced numbers between 1 and 100 are 11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, and 99.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 2:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">low = 120, high = 129<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">1<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>Only 121 is balanced because the sum of digits at even and odd positions are both 2.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 3:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">low = 1234, high = 1234<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">0<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>1234 is not balanced because the sum of digits at odd positions <code>(1 + 3 = 4)<\/code> does not equal the sum at even positions <code>(2 + 4 = 6)<\/code>.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= low &lt;= high &lt;= 10<sup>15<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n", "essence_html": "<div class=\"card\">\n  <p>An integer is <strong>balanced<\/strong> if it has at least two digits and the sum of its digits at odd-numbered positions (1, 3, 5, …) equals the sum of digits at even-numbered positions (2, 4, 6, …), where the leftmost digit is position 1.<\/p>\n  <p>Given <code>[low, high]<\/code> with values up to 10<sup>15<\/sup>, return how many integers in the range are balanced. Single-digit numbers are never balanced regardless of their value.<\/p>\n  <p>The huge range (up to 10<sup>15<\/sup>, i.e. 16 digits) makes brute-force impossible; you need digit dynamic programming on the decimal representation, counting via <code>f(high) − f(low − 1)<\/code>.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════\n     4. WORKED EXAMPLE\n════════════════════════════════════════ -->", "tempting_html": "<div class=\"card\">\n  <h3>1. Brute-force iteration<\/h3>\n  <p>Loop from <code>low<\/code> to <code>high<\/code>, check each number digit by digit. Cost: O((high − low) × 16). Worst case: high = 10<sup>15<\/sup>, range = 10<sup>15<\/sup>, giving <strong>10<sup>15<\/sup> × 16 = 1.6 × 10<sup>16<\/sup> operations<\/strong> → hard TLE; even at 10<sup>9<\/sup> ops/sec this is 18 million seconds.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <h3>2. Sharing the memoization cache across both f() calls<\/h3>\n  <p>Defining one <code>@lru_cache<\/code> dp function outside the closure and calling it for both <code>count_up_to(high)<\/code> and <code>count_up_to(low−1)<\/code> with the same cache. The tight states carry the digit string of the first call as implicit context; when reused for the second call (different upper bound), cached tight entries return answers valid only for the first x, silently producing <strong>wrong answers<\/strong> on the shared entries. Always scope or clear the cache.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <h3>3. Counting leading zeros as position 1<\/h3>\n  <p>A naïve implementation that starts counting the first digit—even if it's 0—as position 1 will assign the wrong parity to all subsequent digits for numbers shorter than the string length of x. For example, if x = \"1000\" and you consider number 99, the leading zeros make you think position 1 is \"0\" (pos 0), making 9 a position-2 (even) digit. This produces wrong counts for all values with fewer digits than x. The fix is to track <code>ndigits<\/code> separately and only begin parity counting once the first non-zero digit is placed.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════\n     9. ACCEPTED ALGORITHM FAMILIES\n════════════════════════════════════════ -->", "manual_url": "manuals/wc0482-Q4-number-of-balanced-integers-in-a-range.html", "models": [{"family": "codex", "attempts": 2, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "deepseek", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "javascript": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 3, "attempts": 3}}}, {"family": "glm", "attempts": 6, "accepted": 6, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "rust": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "kimi", "attempts": 6, "accepted": 6, "langs": {"cpp": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "rust": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "typescript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "minimax", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "qwen", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 4, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 4}}}, {"family": "claude", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 1, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}], "accepted": 29, "attempts": 34, "rate": 0.853}, {"rank": 7, "id": "wc0483-Q4-minimum-cost-to-merge-sorted-lists", "title": "Minimum Cost to Merge Sorted Lists", "difficulty": "Hard", "contest": 483, "position": "Q4", "leetcode_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-cost-to-merge-sorted-lists/", "function": "minMergeCost", "tags": ["Array", "Two Pointers", "Binary Search", "Dynamic Programming", "Bit Manipulation"], "statement_html": "<p>You are given a 2D integer array <code>lists<\/code>, where each <code>lists[i]<\/code> is a non-empty array of integers <strong>sorted<\/strong> in <strong>non-decreasing<\/strong> order.<\/p>\n\n<p>You may <strong>repeatedly<\/strong> choose two lists <code>a = lists[i]<\/code> and <code>b = lists[j]<\/code>, where <code>i != j<\/code>, and merge them. The <strong>cost<\/strong> to merge <code>a<\/code> and <code>b<\/code> is:<\/p>\n\n<p><code>len(a) + len(b) + abs(median(a) - median(b))<\/code>, where <code>len<\/code> and <code>median<\/code> denote the list length and median, respectively.<\/p>\n\n<p>After merging <code>a<\/code> and <code>b<\/code>, remove both <code>a<\/code> and <code>b<\/code> from <code>lists<\/code> and insert the new merged <strong>sorted list<\/strong> in <strong>any<\/strong> position. Repeat merges until only <strong>one<\/strong> list remains.<\/p>\n\n<p>Return an integer denoting the <strong>minimum total cost<\/strong> required to merge all lists into one single sorted list.<\/p>\n\n<p>The <strong>median<\/strong> of an array is the middle element after sorting it in non-decreasing order. If the array has an even number of elements, the median is the left middle element.<\/p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 1:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">lists = [[1,3,5],[2,4],[6,7,8]]<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">18<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>Merge <code>a = [1, 3, 5]<\/code> and <code>b = [2, 4]<\/code>:<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>len(a) = 3<\/code> and <code>len(b) = 2<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>median(a) = 3<\/code> and <code>median(b) = 2<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>cost = len(a) + len(b) + abs(median(a) - median(b)) = 3 + 2 + abs(3 - 2) = 6<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>So <code>lists<\/code> becomes <code>[[1, 2, 3, 4, 5], [6, 7, 8]]<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>Merge <code>a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<\/code> and <code>b = [6, 7, 8]<\/code>:<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>len(a) = 5<\/code> and <code>len(b) = 3<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>median(a) = 3<\/code> and <code>median(b) = 7<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>cost = len(a) + len(b) + abs(median(a) - median(b)) = 5 + 3 + abs(3 - 7) = 12<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>So <code>lists<\/code> becomes <code>[[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]]<\/code>, and total cost is <code>6 + 12 = 18<\/code>.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 2:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">lists = [[1,1,5],[1,4,7,8]]<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">10<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>Merge <code>a = [1, 1, 5]<\/code> and <code>b = [1, 4, 7, 8]<\/code>:<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>len(a) = 3<\/code> and <code>len(b) = 4<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>median(a) = 1<\/code> and <code>median(b) = 4<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>cost = len(a) + len(b) + abs(median(a) - median(b)) = 3 + 4 + abs(1 - 4) = 10<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>So <code>lists<\/code> becomes <code>[[1, 1, 1, 4, 5, 7, 8]]<\/code>, and total cost is 10.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 3:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">lists = [[1],[3]]<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">4<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>Merge <code>a = [1]<\/code> and <code>b = [3]<\/code>:<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>len(a) = 1<\/code> and <code>len(b) = 1<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>median(a) = 1<\/code> and <code>median(b) = 3<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>cost = len(a) + len(b) + abs(median(a) - median(b)) = 1 + 1 + abs(1 - 3) = 4<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>So <code>lists<\/code> becomes <code>[[1, 3]]<\/code>, and total cost is 4.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 4:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">lists = [[1],[1]]<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">2<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>The total cost is <code>len(a) + len(b) + abs(median(a) - median(b)) = 1 + 1 + abs(1 - 1) = 2<\/code>.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>2 &lt;= lists.length &lt;= 12<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= lists[i].length &lt;= 500<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>-10<sup>9<\/sup> &lt;= lists[i][j] &lt;= 10<sup>9<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>lists[i]<\/code> is sorted in non-decreasing order.<\/li>\n\t<li>The <strong>sum<\/strong> of <code>lists[i].length<\/code> will not exceed 2000.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n", "essence_html": "<p>Given <em>n<\/em> ≤ 12 non-empty sorted lists, you may repeatedly pick any two lists <em>a<\/em> and <em>b<\/em>, pay cost <code>len(a) + len(b) + |median(a) − median(b)|<\/code>, and replace them with their merged sorted list. The median is defined as the <strong>left-middle element<\/strong> (index <code>(len−1)//2<\/code>). Find the merge order that minimises the total cost to reduce all lists to one.<\/p>\n  <p>Because any two lists can be merged at any step (not just adjacent ones), and the optimal split at each level is non-obvious, the problem requires searching over all 2<sup>n<\/sup> subset partitions — a classic bitmask DP setup. The small bound <em>n<\/em> ≤ 12 (giving 4096 masks and 531 441 submask transitions via the 3<sup>n<\/sup> identity) makes this tractable.<\/p>\n<\/section>\n\n<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ 4. WORKED EXAMPLE -->\n<section class=\"card\">", "tempting_html": "<div class=\"wrong-box\">\n    <h4>1. Greedy — always merge the cheapest available pair<\/h4>\n    <p><strong>Complexity:<\/strong> O(n<sup>2<\/sup>) merges, each O(N log N) → O(n<sup>2<\/sup>·N·log N) = 144·2000·11 ≈ 3.2M ops — fast enough.<\/p>\n    <p><strong>Why it fails:<\/strong> Merging cheaply now can poison future medians. Example: lists [1],[2],[1000]. Greedy picks [1]+[2] (cost=3), leaving {[1,2],[1000]}; final cost 3+(2+1+998)=1004. But merging [2]+[1000] first (cost=1000), then [1]+[2,1000] (cost=1+2+|1−500|=502), total=1502 — greedy wins here but fails on carefully constructed inputs where the cheapest early merge shifts a median adversely. The median effect is non-linear and cannot be evaluated without trying all splits.<\/p>\n  <\/div>\n\n  <div class=\"wrong-box\">\n    <h4>2. Interval / contiguous-segment DP — dp[i][j] = min cost to merge lists[i..j]<\/h4>\n    <p><strong>Complexity:<\/strong> O(n<sup>3<\/sup>) states and transitions = 12<sup>3<\/sup> = 1728 — trivially fast.<\/p>\n    <p><strong>Why it fails:<\/strong> This only explores merges of contiguous sub-arrays. The optimal strategy can merge non-adjacent lists first (e.g., L0 and L2 before L1), which an interval DP cannot express. Only a bitmask DP over arbitrary subsets is correct.<\/p>\n  <\/div>\n\n  <div class=\"wrong-box\">\n    <h4>3. Bitmask DP but recomputing the median during each transition (no precomputation)<\/h4>\n    <p><strong>Complexity:<\/strong> Each of the 3<sup>n<\/sup>=531 441 transitions would need to find the median of the sub and comp masks by iterating over all elements — O(N) each → 531 441·2000 ≈ <strong>1.06 × 10<sup>9<\/sup><\/strong> operations → TLE (the 2 TLE submissions in this benchmark likely fell into this trap).<\/p>\n    <p><strong>Fix:<\/strong> Precompute <code>med[mask]<\/code> for all 2<sup>n<\/sup> masks before the DP; transitions then use O(1) lookups.<\/p>\n  <\/div>\n<\/section>\n\n<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ 9. ACCEPTED ALGORITHM FAMILIES -->\n<section class=\"card\">", "manual_url": "manuals/wc0483-Q4-minimum-cost-to-merge-sorted-lists.html", "models": [{"family": "glm", "attempts": 4, "accepted": 4, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "kimi", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 7, "langs": {"cpp": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "rust": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "typescript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "minimax", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "deepseek", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 5, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "javascript": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 3}}}, {"family": "qwen", "attempts": 6, "accepted": 4, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 4}}}, {"family": "claude", "attempts": 2, "accepted": 1, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "codex", "attempts": 2, "accepted": 1, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 2}}}], "accepted": 25, "attempts": 31, "rate": 0.806}, {"rank": 8, "id": "wc0484-Q4-maximum-bitwise-and-after-increment-operations", "title": "Maximum Bitwise AND After Increment Operations", "difficulty": "Hard", "contest": 484, "position": "Q4", "leetcode_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-bitwise-and-after-increment-operations/", "function": "maximumAND", "tags": ["Array", "Greedy", "Bit Manipulation", "Sorting"], "statement_html": "<p>You are given an integer array <code>nums<\/code> and two integers <code>k<\/code> and <code>m<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>You may perform <strong>at most<\/strong> <code>k<\/code> operations. In one operation, you may choose any index <code>i<\/code> and <strong>increase<\/strong> <code>nums[i]<\/code> by 1.<\/p>\n\n<p>Return an integer denoting the <strong>maximum<\/strong> possible <strong>bitwise AND<\/strong> of any <strong><span data-keyword=\"subset\">subset<\/span><\/strong> of size <code>m<\/code> after performing up to <code>k<\/code> operations optimally.<\/p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 1:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">nums = [3,1,2], k = 8, m = 2<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">6<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>We need a subset of size <code>m = 2<\/code>. Choose indices <code>[0, 2]<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>Increase <code>nums[0] = 3<\/code> to 6 using 3 operations, and increase <code>nums[2] = 2<\/code> to 6 using 4 operations.<\/li>\n\t<li>The total number of operations used is 7, which is not greater than <code>k = 8<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>The two chosen values become <code>[6, 6]<\/code>, and their bitwise AND is <code>6<\/code>, which is the maximum possible.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 2:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">nums = [1,2,8,4], k = 7, m = 3<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">4<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>We need a subset of size <code>m = 3<\/code>. Choose indices <code>[0, 1, 3]<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>Increase <code>nums[0] = 1<\/code> to 4 using 3 operations, increase <code>nums[1] = 2<\/code> to 4 using 2 operations, and keep <code>nums[3] = 4<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>The total number of operations used is 5, which is not greater than <code>k = 7<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>The three chosen values become <code>[4, 4, 4]<\/code>, and their bitwise AND is 4, which is the maximum possible.​​​​​​​<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 3:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">nums = [1,1], k = 3, m = 2<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">2<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>We need a subset of size <code>m = 2<\/code>. Choose indices <code>[0, 1]<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>Increase both values from 1 to 2 using 1 operation each.<\/li>\n\t<li>The total number of operations used is 2, which is not greater than <code>k = 3<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>The two chosen values become <code>[2, 2]<\/code>, and their bitwise AND is 2, which is the maximum possible.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= n == nums.length &lt;= 5 * 10<sup>4<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= nums[i] &lt;= 10<sup>9<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= k &lt;= 10<sup>9<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= m &lt;= n<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n", "essence_html": "<p>\nGiven an integer array <code>nums<\/code> and budgets <code>k<\/code> (total +1 increments available)\nand <code>m<\/code> (subset size required), choose any m elements -- after optimally spending at most k\nincrements -- so that their bitwise AND is as large as possible.\n<\/p>\n<p>\nBecause AND requires every chosen element to share a bit, maximizing the AND means aligning\nm elements to a common high-bit pattern. Incrementing an element past a bit boundary\n\"rounds it up\" and sets that bit, but also clears lower bits as a carry side-effect --\nso cost is not simply target minus current value.\n<\/p>\n<p>\nThe key insight is that bits can be decided greedily from the most-significant downward:\nonce a high bit is locked in, only candidates that can also set the next lower bit (with\nremaining budget) are worth considering.\n<\/p>\n<\/section>\n\n<!-- ============================================================ 4. WORKED EXAMPLE -->\n<section id=\"worked-example\">", "tempting_html": "<h3>1. Brute-force subset enumeration<\/h3>\n<p>\nTry every C(n, m) subset, compute the minimum cost to align all m elements to the same\nbit pattern, keep the maximum AND. For n = 5*10^4 and m = 2:\nC(5*10^4, 2) = ~1.25*10^9 pairs. Even O(1) per pair is ~1.25*10^9 ops -- 10x over TLE.\nFor m = 3: ~2.1*10^13 triples.\n<\/p>\n\n<h3>2. Binary search on the integer answer value<\/h3>\n<p>\nBinary search over [0, 2^31] with a can_achieve(T) feasibility predicate --\n31 iterations of O(n log n) = ~2.5*10^7 ops, comfortably fast.\n<b>But it is incorrect<\/b> because can_achieve(T) is not monotone in T.\nCounterexample: nums = [5], k = 0, m = 1.\ncan_achieve(5) = True (5 already has all bits of 5), but can_achieve(3) = False\n(reaching 3's bit 1 from 5 requires incrementing 5 to 6, cost 1 &gt; 0).\nBinary search exploring mid=3 marks False and contracts hi=2; it returns 1 instead of 5.\nAmong the 17 accepted solutions, the two glm-5 files use this approach and happened to\npass LeetCode's provided test suite -- but this does not establish correctness on all\nvalid inputs per the given constraints.\n<\/p>\n\n<h3>3. Greedy: sort nums descending, increment the top m<\/h3>\n<p>\nPick the m largest elements and spend all k increments on them, hoping the AND converges.\nFails because bit alignment matters more than raw value. A small element very close to a\nbit boundary may converge to the optimal AND target far cheaper than a large element\nmisaligned with that boundary. Example: nums = [7, 8], k = 1, m = 2. The \"top 2\" are\nboth chosen. AND(7, 8) = 0, and one increment cannot help. But with a targeted approach,\nno meaningful AND is achievable anyway -- but in larger examples, alignment-aware subset\nselection changes the winner entirely.\n<\/p>\n<\/section>\n\n<!-- ============================================================ 9. ACCEPTED ALGORITHM FAMILIES -->\n<section id=\"accepted-algorithm-families\">", "manual_url": "manuals/wc0484-Q4-maximum-bitwise-and-after-increment-operations.html", "models": [{"family": "codex", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 3, "attempts": 3}}}, {"family": "minimax", "attempts": 2, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "deepseek", "attempts": 6, "accepted": 5, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 3}}}, {"family": "glm", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "kimi", "attempts": 6, "accepted": 4, "langs": {"cpp": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "typescript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "qwen", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "claude", "attempts": 2, "accepted": 1, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}], "accepted": 19, "attempts": 25, "rate": 0.76}, {"rank": 9, "id": "wc0489-Q3-longest-almost-palindromic-substring", "title": "Longest Almost-Palindromic Substring", "difficulty": "Medium", "contest": 489, "position": "Q3", "leetcode_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-almost-palindromic-substring/", "function": "almostPalindromic", "tags": ["Two Pointers", "String", "Dynamic Programming"], "statement_html": "<p>You are given a string <code>s<\/code> consisting of lowercase English letters.<\/p>\n\n<p>A <span data-keyword=\"substring-nonempty\">substring<\/span> is <strong>almost-palindromic<\/strong> if it becomes a <span data-keyword=\"palindrome-string\">palindrome<\/span> after removing <strong>exactly<\/strong> one character from it.<\/p>\n\n<p>Return an integer denoting the length of the <strong>longest<\/strong> <strong>almost-palindromic<\/strong> substring in <code>s<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 1:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">s = &quot;abca&quot;<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">4<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>Choose the substring <code>&quot;<u><strong>abca<\/strong><\/u>&quot;<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Remove <code>&quot;ab<u><strong>c<\/strong><\/u>a&quot;<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>The string becomes <code>&quot;aba&quot;<\/code>, which is a palindrome.<\/li>\n\t<li>Therefore, <code>&quot;abca&quot;<\/code> is almost-palindromic.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 2:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">s = &quot;abba&quot;<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">4<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>Choose the substring <code>&quot;<u><strong>abba<\/strong><\/u>&quot;<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Remove <code>&quot;a<u><strong>b<\/strong><\/u>ba&quot;<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>The string becomes <code>&quot;aba&quot;<\/code>, which is a palindrome.<\/li>\n\t<li>Therefore, <code>&quot;abba&quot;<\/code> is almost-palindromic.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 3:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">s = &quot;zzabba&quot;<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">5<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>Choose the substring <code>&quot;z<u><strong>zabba<\/strong><\/u>&quot;<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Remove <code>&quot;<u><strong>z<\/strong><\/u>abba&quot;<\/code>.<\/li>\n\t<li>The string becomes <code>&quot;abba&quot;<\/code>, which is a palindrome.<\/li>\n\t<li>Therefore, <code>&quot;zabba&quot;<\/code> is almost-palindromic.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>2 &lt;= s.length &lt;= 2500<\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>s<\/code> consists of only lowercase English letters.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n", "essence_html": "<p>\nGiven a string <code>s<\/code>, find the length of the longest substring that becomes a palindrome\nafter removing <em>exactly one<\/em> character from it. Every palindrome of length 2 or more already\nqualifies because you can always remove one character and leave a palindrome. The task is to find\nsubstrings that are \"one deletion away\" from being a palindrome, and return the maximum length.\nWith <code>n &lt;= 2500<\/code>, an O(n^2) solution is the target complexity.\n<\/p>\n<\/section>\n\n<!-- 4. Worked Example -->\n<section class=\"section\">", "tempting_html": "<div class=\"warn-box\">\n<h3>Approach 1: Brute-force O(n^3) check<\/h3>\n<p>For each substring s[i..j], try removing each character k in [i,j] and check if the remaining string is a palindrome.<\/p>\n<p>Complexity: O(n^2) substrings * O(n) removals * O(n) palindrome check = <strong>O(n^4)<\/strong>.\nWith n=2500: 2500^4 / something... even O(n^3) = 2500^3 = 15.6e9 ops, far over the ~10^8 limit.\nEven with hashing to make each check O(1), O(n^3) = 15.6e9 is TLE.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"warn-box\">\n<h3>Approach 2: Expand-around-center but only try skip once (boundary only)<\/h3>\n<p>Only add +1 when one pointer runs off the edge of the string, missing interior mismatches\nwhere skipping either side and continuing expansion would yield a longer result.<\/p>\n<p>This gives wrong answers: for <code>\"zzabba\"<\/code>, without re-expanding after the skip,\nthe algorithm would not find that skipping 'z' at position 1 and then finding \"abba\" gives length 5.\nInterior skip + continuation is essential.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"warn-box\">\n<h3>Approach 3: DP where dp[i][j] stores the full deletion set<\/h3>\n<p>Storing which characters to delete (not just the minimum count) to check exact-1-deletion.\nMemory: O(n^2) entries each holding a set -&gt; O(n^3) total. With n=2500: 2500^2 * 2500 =\n15.6e9 bytes. Completely infeasible. The minimum-count scalar <code>dp[i][j]<\/code> is all\nthat is needed; checking <code>dp[i][j] &lt;= 1<\/code> is sufficient.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/section>\n\n<!-- 9. Accepted Algorithm Families -->\n<section class=\"section\">", "manual_url": "manuals/wc0489-Q3-longest-almost-palindromic-substring.html", "models": [{"family": "deepseek", "attempts": 6, "accepted": 5, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "javascript": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "minimax", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "glm", "attempts": 5, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "rust": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "qwen", "attempts": 4, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "kimi", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 3, "langs": {"cpp": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "golang": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "rust": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "typescript": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "claude", "attempts": 2, "accepted": 0, "langs": {"python3": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 2}}}], "accepted": 15, "attempts": 27, "rate": 0.556}, {"rank": 10, "id": "wc0485-Q4-lexicographically-smallest-string-after-deleting-duplicate-characters", "title": "Lexicographically Smallest String After Deleting Duplicate Characters", "difficulty": "Hard", "contest": 485, "position": "Q4", "leetcode_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/lexicographically-smallest-string-after-deleting-duplicate-characters/", "function": "lexSmallestAfterDeletion", "tags": ["Hash Table", "String", "Stack", "Greedy", "Monotonic Stack"], "statement_html": "<p>You are given a string <code>s<\/code> that consists of lowercase English letters.<\/p>\n\n<p>You can perform the following operation any number of times (possibly zero times):<\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Choose any letter that appears <strong>at least twice<\/strong> in the current string <code>s<\/code> and delete any <strong>one<\/strong> occurrence.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p>Return the <strong><span data-keyword=\"lexicographically-smaller-string\">lexicographically smallest<\/span><\/strong> resulting string that can be formed this way.<\/p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 1:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">s = &quot;aaccb&quot;<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">&quot;aacb&quot;<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>We can form the strings <code>&quot;acb&quot;<\/code>, <code>&quot;aacb&quot;<\/code>, <code>&quot;accb&quot;<\/code>, and <code>&quot;aaccb&quot;<\/code>. <code>&quot;aacb&quot;<\/code> is the lexicographically smallest one.<\/p>\n\n<p>For example, we can obtain <code>&quot;aacb&quot;<\/code> by choosing <code>&#39;c&#39;<\/code> and deleting its first occurrence.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p><strong class=\"example\">Example 2:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div class=\"example-block\">\n<p><strong>Input:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">s = &quot;z&quot;<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Output:<\/strong> <span class=\"example-io\">&quot;z&quot;<\/span><\/p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<p>We cannot perform any operations. The only string we can form is <code>&quot;z&quot;<\/code>.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= s.length &lt;= 10<sup>5<\/sup><\/code><\/li>\n\t<li><code>s<\/code> contains lowercase English letters only.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n", "essence_html": "<p>\n  Given a string <code>s<\/code> of lowercase letters, you may repeatedly delete one copy\n  of any letter that currently appears more than once. The operation preserves order and\n  can be applied any number of times. Return the lexicographically smallest string\n  reachable by any sequence of such deletions.\n<\/p>\n<p>\n  Every distinct letter in <code>s<\/code> must appear at least once in the result --\n  you can only delete a letter if a duplicate remains. Because duplicates provide\n  \"free\" deletions, the optimal strategy picks characters greedily from left to right,\n  always choosing the smallest letter whose selection still leaves every other\n  required letter reachable later in the string.\n<\/p>\n\n<!-- ============================================================ -->\n<!-- 4. WORKED EXAMPLE                                            -->\n<!-- ============================================================ -->", "tempting_html": "<div class=\"warn\">\n  <strong>Approach 1: Apply \"Remove Duplicate Letters\" (LC 316) directly.<\/strong><br>\n  LC 316 builds the lexicographically smallest subsequence with exactly one copy of each letter.\n  For <code>\"aaccb\"<\/code> that gives <code>\"acb\"<\/code>, but the correct answer is <code>\"aacb\"<\/code>.\n  The difference: here we may keep multiple copies of a letter when doing so produces a\n  lexicographically smaller prefix. The constraint is only that we cannot reduce any letter below 1\n  copy, not that we must reduce it to exactly 1. Complexity of the wrong approach: O(n) time,\n  but systematically produces Wrong Answer whenever an extra copy of a small letter appears\n  before a needed larger letter.\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"warn\">\n  <strong>Approach 2: Greedy scan for globally smallest char with no limit constraint.<\/strong><br>\n  At each step, scan <code>s[cur:]<\/code> for the smallest character and pick its first occurrence.\n  This ignores the deadline: for <code>\"bac\"<\/code>, need={a,b,c}; picking 'a' at index 1 sets\n  cur=2, but 'b' only exists at index 0 &lt; cur -- it can never be placed. Result: wrong or\n  infinite loop. Complexity: O(n) per step, O(26) steps = O(26n) ~ O(n). Fast but wrong;\n  14+ Wrong Answer verdicts in this benchmark confirm the feasibility check is mandatory.\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"warn\">\n  <strong>Approach 3: O(n^2) naive feasibility check per candidate position.<\/strong><br>\n  At each of the at most n picks, for each candidate position j scan s[j:] (O(n)) to verify\n  every needed char is still present. Total: O(n) candidates * O(n) scan = O(n^2).\n  For n = 10^5: 10^5 * 10^5 = 10^10 ops -- TLE. The fix is to precompute either\n  <code>suffix_mask[i]<\/code> (bitmask, O(26n) space) or <code>last[c]<\/code> (O(26) space)\n  so each feasibility check is O(26) rather than O(n).\n<\/div>\n\n<!-- ============================================================ -->\n<!-- 9. ACCEPTED ALGORITHM FAMILIES                               -->\n<!-- ============================================================ -->", "manual_url": "manuals/wc0485-Q4-lexicographically-smallest-string-after-deleting-duplicate-characters.html", "models": [{"family": "codex", "attempts": 2, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "deepseek", "attempts": 5, "accepted": 4, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 2, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "minimax", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 2, "langs": {"golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "claude", "attempts": 3, "accepted": 1, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "qwen", "attempts": 4, "accepted": 1, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 2}, "python3": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 2}}}, {"family": "glm", "attempts": 5, "accepted": 1, "langs": {"java": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 2}, "rust": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}}}, {"family": "kimi", "attempts": 7, "accepted": 1, "langs": {"cpp": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "golang": {"accepted": 1, "attempts": 1}, "java": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "javascript": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "python3": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "rust": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}, "typescript": {"accepted": 0, "attempts": 1}}}], "accepted": 12, "attempts": 29, "rate": 0.414}];
const KEY = 'hardhalf:attempts';
const load = () => { try { return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) || '{}'); } catch (e) { return {}; } };
const save = (o) => { try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(o)); } catch (e) {} };
const fmt = (ms) => { const s = Math.floor(ms / 1000); return String(Math.floor(s / 60)).padStart(2, '0') + ':' + String(s % 60).padStart(2, '0'); };
const esc = (t) => t.replace(/[&<>"]/g, c => ({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;'}[c]));
function chip(m) {
  const cls = m.accepted === m.attempts ? 'all' : (m.accepted === 0 ? 'none' : '');
  const langs = Object.entries(m.langs).map(([l, v]) => l + ' ' + v.accepted + '/' + v.attempts).join(', ');
  return `<span class="chip ${cls}" title="${esc(langs)}">${esc(m.family)} ${m.accepted}/${m.attempts}</span>`;
}
function render() {
  const att = load();
  document.getElementById('ladder').innerHTML = DATA.map(p => {
    const a = att[p.id];
    const pct = Math.round(100 * p.rate);
    const failedFams = p.models.filter(m => m.accepted < m.attempts).map(m => m.family);
    return `<li class="card${a ? ' done' : ''}" data-id="${p.id}">
<header><span class="rank">RUNG ${p.rank}</span><h2>${esc(p.title)}</h2><span class="diff">${esc(p.difficulty)}</span><span class="note">Weekly Contest ${p.contest || ''} · ${esc(p.position || '')}</span></header>
<p class="challenge">${p.accepted} of ${p.attempts} model submissions were accepted (${pct}%).${failedFams.length ? ' Stumbled: ' + failedFams.map(esc).join(', ') + '.' : ' Nobody stumbled.'} Can you?</p>
<div class="bar"><i style="width:${pct}%"></i></div>
<div class="chips">${p.models.map(chip).join('')}</div>
<details><summary>Problem statement</summary><div class="statement">${p.statement_html}</div><p class="note"><a href="${p.leetcode_url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open on LeetCode</a> · function <code>${esc(p.function || '')}</code></p></details>
<div class="attempt">
<div class="controls"><button class="primary" data-act="start">Start</button><span class="clock" data-clock>00:00</span><button data-act="pass" disabled>Passed</button><button data-act="fail" disabled>Failed</button><button data-act="quit" disabled>Gave up</button></div>
<p class="note">Solve it anywhere. The clock is for you, not for us.</p>
</div>
<div class="reveal">
<p class="verdict">${a ? `You recorded <b>${esc(a.verdict)}</b> after ${fmt(a.ms)} on ${new Date(a.at).toLocaleDateString()}. <button data-act="reset">Try again</button>` : ''}</p>
<h3>What the models got wrong here</h3>
<div class="tempting">${p.tempting_html || '<p class="note">No section available for this problem.</p>'}</div>
<h3>The manual</h3>
<p>Built from every accepted model solution: the essence, the algorithm families that worked, and the failure patterns from the stats. <a href="${p.manual_url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the manual for ${esc(p.title)}</a></p>
</div>
</li>`;
  }).join('');
  const done = Object.values(att);
  const passed = done.filter(x => x.verdict === 'passed').length;
  document.getElementById('summary').textContent = done.length ? `${done.length} of ${DATA.length} rungs attempted, ${passed} passed. Median time ${fmt(median(done.map(x => x.ms)))}.` : 'No attempts recorded yet in this browser.';
}
function median(xs) { const s = [...xs].sort((a, b) => a - b); return s.length ? (s.length % 2 ? s[(s.length - 1) / 2] : (s[s.length / 2 - 1] + s[s.length / 2]) / 2) : 0; }
const timers = {};
document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const b = e.target.closest('button[data-act]'); if (!b) return;
  const card = b.closest('.card'); const id = card.dataset.id; const act = b.dataset.act;
  if (act === 'start') {
    timers[id] = { t0: Date.now(), iv: setInterval(() => { card.querySelector('[data-clock]').textContent = fmt(Date.now() - timers[id].t0); }, 500) };
    card.querySelectorAll('button[data-act]').forEach(x => x.disabled = x.dataset.act === 'start');
  } else if (act === 'reset') {
    const att = load(); delete att[id]; save(att); render();
  } else {
    const t = timers[id]; if (!t) return; clearInterval(t.iv);
    const att = load(); att[id] = { verdict: { pass: 'passed', fail: 'failed', quit: 'gave up' }[act], ms: Date.now() - t.t0, at: Date.now() }; save(att); delete timers[id]; render();
    document.querySelector(`.card[data-id="${id}"]`).scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
  }
});
render();
</script>
</body>
</html>

exec
/bin/bash -lc "python3 - <<'PY'
import json,re,html
s=open('docs/index.html').read()
data=json.loads(re.search(r'const DATA = (.*);\\nconst KEY',s).group(1))
